description:
Background:
Redwood SDKs currently surface errors inconsistently across Python, TypeScript, and Go. Engineers building on top of multiple SDKs must implement duplication logic to translate low-level transport/HTTP failures, runtime token-decoding errors, and server-side model degradation signals into consistent, actionable responses for caller code or UI. This ticket defines a cross-SDK design and rollout plan that standardizes: canonical error codes, structured metadata for telemetry and retries, user-facing message templates, and ergonomics for resumable streaming RPCs.

Goals:
- Define a compact canonical error code set (client, transient, server, quota, auth, model-quality) and mapping rules from HTTP/gRPC/internal runtime codes per-language.
- Introduce a structured Error object (error.code, error.subcode, error.hint, error.retryable, error.retry_after_ms, error.trace_id) that is available in exceptions/rejections across SDKs.
- Add SDK-level helper primitives for retries (exponential backoff helper) and a best-effort shallow resume token pattern for streaming endpoints (chat/streaming generation).
- Instrument SDKs to emit consistent telemetry fields for each error (redwood_error_code, retry_hint, model_variant, latency_ms) to simplify downstream alerting and SLO correlation.

Design summary (cross-language):
- Canonical codes: RDW_CLIENT, RDW_TRANSIENT, RDW_AUTH, RDW_QUOTA, RDW_MODEL_DEGRADATION, RDW_RUNTIME, RDW_UNKNOWN. Each maps to HTTP/gRPC codes and an optional subcode string (eg. model_timeout, kv_cache_miss).
- Error surface:
  - Python: raise RedwoodError with attributes (code, subcode, hint, retryable: bool, retry_after_ms: int|None, metadata: dict, trace_id).
  - TypeScript: throw new RedwoodError({ code, subcode, hint, retryable, retryAfterMs, metadata, traceId }). Promise rejections should carry the same shape.
  - Go: return (*redwood.Error) which implements Error() and exposes accessor methods: Code(), Subcode(), Hint(), Retryable(), RetryAfterMs(), Metadata(), TraceID().
- Streaming ergonomics: all streaming response objects include a ResumeToken header when available. SDK streaming helpers should expose a .resume() method that accepts a resume token and attempts to continue from the last confirmed output. Resume is best-effort and documented as non-guaranteed across model variants.
- Retry guidance: SDKs expose a RetryPolicy utility with recommended defaults per canonical code (eg. RDW_TRANSIENT => retryable true, backoff base 200ms, max_attempts 5; RDW_QUOTA => retryable false unless server returns explicit retry_after_ms). The SDK should never retry idempotent-only assumptions automatically for streaming sessions without explicit user opt-in.

Acceptance criteria:
- Language-specific SDKs implement the standardized Error shape and map existing transport/library errors to canonical codes for >=90% of observed incidents in error telemetry.
- Streaming helpers in Python/TS/Go return tokens in successful streams and accept resume tokens; integration test demonstrates resume for a short sequence.
- SDK telemetry logs include the specified fields and a small sample pipeline validates consistent code values across languages.
- A migration doc and quickstart example for Python/TS/Go published to the SDK repo and internal docs (Confluence) plus two example snippets for handling backoff + resume.

Non-goals:
- Not changing server-side error contract beyond adding optional resume token header and consistent subcode strings. Server changes will be coordinated separately.

Implementation notes and concerns:
- Backwards compatibility: SDKs must continue to expose existing exception types for simple catch-all flows, but recommend switching to RedwoodError for rich handling. We will keep a compatibility adapter that wraps old exceptions into RedwoodError until the next major bump.
- Security/PII: No user content should be copied into hint fields; hints are templated and reference internal constants or safe metadata only.
- Observability: decide whether trace_id is the server trace or SDK generated id; initial implementation will surface both: sdk_trace_id (generated) and server_trace_id (if returned). For a flat schema we put server_trace_id as metadata entry.

Examples (illustrative):
- Python snippet:
  try:
      client.generate(...)
  except RedwoodError as e:
      if e.code == 'RDW_TRANSIENT' and e.retryable:
          sleep(e.retry_after_ms / 1000 or 0.2)
          # use exponential backoff helper: backoff.next()
      elif e.code == 'RDW_MODEL_DEGRADATION':
          log.warn(e.hint)
          fallback_to_model('gpt-lite')
      else:
          raise

- TypeScript snippet:
  try {
    await client.chat.create(...)
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.code === 'RDW_QUOTA') {
      showBillingModal()
    } else if (e.code === 'RDW_TRANSIENT' && e.retryable) {
      await retryWithPolicy(() => client.chat.create(...), e.retryAfterMs)
    } else {
      throw e
    }
  }

- Go snippet:
  resp, err := client.Generate(ctx, req)
  if err != nil {
    if e, ok := err.(*redwood.Error); ok {
      if e.Retryable() {
        time.Sleep(time.Duration(e.RetryAfterMs()) * time.Millisecond)
        // retry logic
      }
    }
  }

Rollout plan:
- Phase 1 (2 weeks): Finalize canonical codes and subcodes, implement Error shape in Python sdk and add telemetry fields. Add unit tests and mapping layer for HTTP errors.
- Phase 2 (3 weeks): Implement in TypeScript and Go, add streaming resume token header parsing, implement resume helper in all SDKs.
- Phase 3 (1 week): Publish migration docs, examples, and coordinate with server runtime team to standardize subcode values.

Notes and open decisions (short):
- Should the canonical code set include a separate RDW_RATE_LIMIT vs RDW_QUOTA? Currently we group them under RDW_QUOTA but server may prefer split.
- Resume token shape: base64 encoded JSON vs opaque string. Prefer opaque string to avoid leak of internals.

Documentation links: Confluence: https://confluence.internal.redwood.ai/display/SDK/Canonical+Error+Codes (draft)
Design doc PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-design/pull/89
